# 8. Module Overview

Every package starts with a short description.

Example:

```
Reward Module

Purpose:

Maintain the immutable Reward Ledger and publish Reward-related Business Events.

This module owns all Reward calculations.

Other modules may never calculate Reward balances.
```

---

# 9. Business Responsibilities

Every package explicitly defines:

The module is responsible for:

- ...

- ...

- ...

Responsibilities should reference Blueprint documents whenever possible.

---

# 10. Non-Responsibilities

Every package explicitly defines what the module must never do.

Example:

Reward Module

Not responsible for:

- Status calculation

- Notifications

- Automation execution

- Customer authentication

This section prevents responsibility creep.

---

# 11. Blueprint References

Every package lists only the Blueprint documents required for implementation.

Example:

```
33-domain-model-v2.md

36-event-storming-purchase-processing.md

38-event-storming-redemption.md

42-data-model-v1.md

43-api-contract.md

44-permission-matrix.md
```

Historical documents should not be included.

---

# 12. Engineering References

Every package references only the required Engineering documents.

Typical references include:

- Engineering Implementation Guide
- Repository Structure
- Definition of Done
- Coding Standards
- UAT
- Knowledge Map

Only documents required for implementation should be included.

---

# 13. Domain Objects

Each package identifies its owned entities.

Example:

Reward Module

Reward Transaction

Reward Ledger

Reward Projection

Reward Reservation

Reward Expiration

Objects owned by other modules must not be listed as owned.

---

# 14. Aggregate Ownership

Every package identifies:

Owned Aggregates

Referenced Aggregates

Forbidden Aggregates

Example:

Reward owns:

Reward Ledger

Reward Reservation

Reward Projection

Reward references:

Membership

Customer

Receipt

Reward never owns:

Status

Benefit

Automation

---

# 15. Database Objects

Every package lists:

Tables

Views

Functions

Triggers

Indexes

Policies

owned by the module.

No cross-module ownership is permitted.

---

# 16. API Ownership

Every package identifies:

REST endpoints

RPC endpoints

Internal APIs

owned by the module.

Public APIs should be distinguished from internal interfaces.

---

# 17. Command Ownership

Every package lists every Command it owns.

Example:

CreateRewardTransaction

ReserveRewardBalance

ConfirmRewardRedemption

ReleaseRewardReservation

Only the owning module may implement these Commands.

---

# 18. Query Ownership

Every package lists all supported Queries.

Queries should be read-only.

Examples:

GetRewardBalance

GetRewardHistory

GetRewardReservations

---

# 19. Event Ownership

Every package identifies:

Events Published

Events Consumed

Events Ignored

Event ownership must remain consistent with the Event Catalog.

---

# 20. Permission Requirements

Every package identifies:

Required Roles

Tenant Validation

RLS Expectations

Authorization Rules

Permission Matrix references

Security requirements remain module-specific.

---

# 21. Module Dependencies

Dependencies are classified as:

Required

Optional

Forbidden

Example:

Reward

Requires:

Membership

Receipt

Forbidden:

Status

Benefit

Automation

Dependencies must never create circular ownership.

---

# 22. Allowed Files

Every package explicitly lists which folders may be modified.

Example:

```
services/api/src/modules/reward/**

database/migrations/**

tests/reward/**
```

Agents may modify only these files unless the task explicitly expands the scope.

---

# 23. Forbidden Files

Every package explicitly identifies protected areas.

Example:

```
status/**

automation/**

notification/**

customer/**
```

Changing forbidden files requires a separate implementation task.

---

# 24. Mandatory Tests

Each package lists mandatory:

Unit Tests

Integration Tests

API Tests

Concurrency Tests

Security Tests

Performance Tests

Regression Tests

No package may be merged without completing its mandatory tests.

---

# 25. UAT References

Every package references applicable UAT scenarios.

Example:

Reward Module

UAT-RWD-001

UAT-RWD-002

UAT-SEC-004

UAT-REL-003

This provides direct traceability between implementation and validation.

---

# 26. Definition of Done

Every package references:

55-module-definition-of-done.md

Package-specific completion criteria may extend, but never weaken, the standard Definition of Done.

---

# 27. Review Requirements

Every package defines mandatory reviewers.

Typical reviewers:

Backend Review

Database Review

Security Review

Architecture Review

QA Review

Documentation Review

Review requirements depend on module risk.

---

# 28. AI Context Rules

Implementation agents should load:

- the assigned Module Implementation Package;
- referenced Blueprint documents;
- referenced Engineering documents.

Agents should not load unrelated documentation unless required by dependencies.

This minimizes context size while preserving implementation quality.

---

# 29. Package Maintenance

Module Implementation Packages are maintained by the Solution Architect.

Implementation Agents may propose updates.

Changes require Architecture Review.

---

# 30. Definition of Module Implementation Package Ready

A Module Implementation Package is considered ready when:

- module ownership is defined;
- Blueprint references are complete;
- Engineering references are complete;
- responsibilities are defined;
- non-responsibilities are defined;
- aggregate ownership is defined;
- APIs are identified;
- Commands are identified;
- Queries are identified;
- Events are identified;
- database ownership is defined;
- permission requirements are defined;
- dependencies are documented;
- allowed files are defined;
- forbidden files are defined;
- mandatory tests are identified;
- UAT references exist;
- Definition of Done is referenced;
- review requirements are defined;
- AI context rules are documented.

A completed Module Implementation Package becomes the authoritative implementation context for all Tasks assigned to the corresponding module.